UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 9, Lesson 3: Technical language', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Technical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nDefine condensation in one learner-friendly sentence and give one everyday example.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCondensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass.\n\nCHECK\nUsing a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nBuild a five-word glossary for your explanation topic. For each: plain definition, example sentence and a non-example. Note where you checked meanings.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Technical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Build a five-word glossary for your explanation topic. For each: plain definition, example sentence and a non-example. Note where you checked meanings. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass. Reasoning: I used technical language by attending to definition rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. Correction: Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass. Why: the correction restores accurate use of technical language.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating technical language on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with technical language, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass. This matches the lesson skill for technical language.","options":["Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass."],"question":"Define condensation in one learner-friendly sentence and give one everyday example. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass.","Careful use of definition with clear evidence.","Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert.","Technical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising technical language?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named definition, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the definition used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising technical language. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries technical language.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show technical language and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses technical language from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about technical language?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise definition?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about technical language.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of technical language to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating technical language on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass. Reasoning: I used technical language by attending to definition rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with technical language, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. Correction: Condensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass. Why: the correction restores accurate use of technical language.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w09-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise technical language.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use technical language with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify definition and explain why it matters.","I can practise example independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Technical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Technical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about technical language?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Evaporation is when liquid water becomes vapour. Heat from the sun can cause evaporation from a puddle.\" Definition, then use.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDefine condensation in one learner-friendly sentence and give one everyday example.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCondensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass.","explain":"Technical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Define technical terms before relying on them.","caption":"Audience-friendly wording first.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Define</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">evaporation in plain English</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Then use</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sun heats the puddle</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"Evaporation is when liquid water becomes vapour. Heat from the sun can cause evaporation from a puddle.\" Definition, th Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"Evaporation is when liquid water becomes vapour. Heat from the sun can cause evaporation from a puddle.\" Definition, th Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about technical language?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Evaporation is when liquid water becomes vapour. Heat from the sun can cause evaporation from a puddle.\" Definition, then use. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Glossary entry shape contrasted with undefined jargon.","caption":"Check meanings in a source.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Glossary</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">definition + example + non-example</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">long word, no meaning</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about technical language?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. Here is a more productive model: \"Evaporation is when liquid water becomes vapour. Heat from the sun can cause evaporation from a puddle.\" Definition, then use.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCondensation is when vapour cools into liquid; example: water drops on a cold glass.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Using a long word without a definition can block understanding even if it sounds expert. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about technical language?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTechnical language names subject ideas precisely; define terms in plain English before relying on them for a Year 4 audience. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 9, Lesson 3: Technical language","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Explanations, news and digital texts","week":9,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"Evaporation is when liquid water becomes vapour. Heat from the sun can cause evaporation from a puddle.\" Definition, then use.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Define technical terms before relying on them.","caption":"Audience-friendly wording first.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/s